1
00:00:08,750 --> 00:00:09,880
Hey, Eddie. Aah!

2
00:00:12,090 --> 00:00:14,380
What are you doing here?

3
00:00:12,090 --> 00:00:14,380
Watching you sleep.

4
00:00:16,260 --> 00:00:17,340
Why?

5
00:00:17,510 --> 00:00:19,560
It makes me feel, um, peaceful.

6
00:00:19,720 --> 00:00:21,390
Ha, ha. Ahem. Please.

7
00:00:22,230 --> 00:00:23,520
I can't sleep now!

8
00:00:23,690 --> 00:00:24,890
Oh, you want me to sing?

9
00:00:25,850 --> 00:00:27,520
No. Look.

10
00:00:27,690 --> 00:00:31,030
That's it. I want you out of the apartment now!

11
00:00:31,190 --> 00:00:33,280
Oh, what are you talking about?

12
00:00:33,450 --> 00:00:34,820
Hannibal Lecter?

13
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:37,610
Better roommate than you.

14
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:38,820
No.

15
00:00:38,990 --> 00:00:42,870
I don't think you're being fair! One night you see me, you get scared.

16
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:46,000
What about the other nights when you don't see me, huh?

17
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:48,960
Last night you went and got some water...

18
00:00:49,210 --> 00:00:52,630
and I was nice enough to hide behind the door. What that about, huh?

19
00:00:53,050 --> 00:00:55,170
I didn't realize that.

20
00:00:56,380 --> 00:00:57,470
Get out now!

21
00:00:57,640 --> 00:01:00,220
Okay. You really want me out?

22
00:01:00,390 --> 00:01:01,470
Yes, please.

23
00:01:01,640 --> 00:01:04,310
Then I want to hear you say you want me out.

24
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:05,770
I want you out.

25
00:01:05,940 --> 00:01:07,640
I want to hear it from your lips.

26
00:01:09,190 --> 00:01:10,560
Where'd you hear it before?

27
00:01:10,730 --> 00:01:13,820
Oh, right, all right. You know what? Consider me gone.

28
00:01:14,070 --> 00:01:17,320
I'll be out by the time you get home from work tomorrow.

29
00:01:18,870 --> 00:01:20,870
Thank you.

30
00:01:18,870 --> 00:01:20,870
I heard that.

31
00:02:13,380 --> 00:02:14,460
Thanks.

32
00:02:14,630 --> 00:02:15,710
Hey.

33
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:17,420
Well, look at you!

34
00:02:17,670 --> 00:02:19,470
Finally got that time machine working, huh?

35
00:02:20,970 --> 00:02:22,090
You like it?

36
00:02:22,260 --> 00:02:24,970
This guy was selling them on 8th Avenue and I thought:

37
00:02:25,220 --> 00:02:28,100
"You know what I don't have?"

38
00:02:25,220 --> 00:02:28,100
A mirror?

39
00:02:29,270 --> 00:02:32,020
Fine, make fun. I think it's jaunty.

40
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:36,940
Wow! For a guy who's recently lost his job, you're in a good mood.

41
00:02:37,110 --> 00:02:40,280
I'll be all right. I'm not starting from square one.

42
00:02:40,530 --> 00:02:43,950
I was Dr. Drake Ramoray on Days of our Lives.

43
00:02:44,120 --> 00:02:46,950
Huh? That's got to have some kind of cachet.

44
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:50,160
Cachet? Jaunty?

45
00:02:50,330 --> 00:02:53,250
Chandler gave me word-of-the-day toilet paper.

46
00:02:55,170 --> 00:02:56,670
I'm gonna get some coffee.

47
00:02:57,880 --> 00:02:59,050
Hey.

48
00:02:59,220 --> 00:03:01,680
Oh! So? Did you read the book?

49
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:04,090
Oh, my God. It was incredible!

50
00:03:04,260 --> 00:03:05,800
Didn't it, like, totally speak to you?

51
00:03:05,970 --> 00:03:08,020
Whoa, Whoa, What book is this?

52
00:03:08,180 --> 00:03:11,890
Uh! You have to read this book. It's called Be Your Own Wind Keeper.

53
00:03:12,060 --> 00:03:14,980
Ooh. It's about how women need to become more empowered.

54
00:03:15,150 --> 00:03:17,650
And, oh! But there's wind.

55
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:22,070
And the wind can make us goddesses.

56
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:25,120
Do you know who takes our wind? Men. They just take it.

57
00:03:27,790 --> 00:03:29,580
Men just take our wind?

58
00:03:29,790 --> 00:03:33,750
Uh-huh. All the time. Because they are the lightning-bearers.

59
00:03:34,130 --> 00:03:36,750
Wow.

60
00:03:36,920 --> 00:03:39,710
That sounds kind of cool. It's like The Hobbit.

61
00:03:40,510 --> 00:03:42,090
It is nothing like The Hobbit.

62
00:03:43,260 --> 00:03:45,970
It's like reading about every relationship I've ever had.

63
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:47,470
Except for Richard.

64
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:49,640
Oh, yes. No. Richard would never steal your wind.

65
00:03:49,810 --> 00:03:51,230
No.

66
00:03:51,390 --> 00:03:53,810
Because he's yummy.

67
00:03:51,390 --> 00:03:53,810
Yes.

68
00:03:53,980 --> 00:03:55,150
But all the other ones.

69
00:03:55,310 --> 00:03:58,190
Oh, and the part about how they're always drinking...

70
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:00,280
from our pool of inner power.

71
00:04:00,530 --> 00:04:02,740
But God forbid we should take a sip.

72
00:04:02,990 --> 00:04:04,110
Anybody want a cruller?

73
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:07,700
This is a typical lightning-bearer thing.

74
00:04:07,870 --> 00:04:09,530
Right there. It's like, um...

75
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:13,410
Hello. Who wants one of my phallic-shaped man-cakes?

76
00:04:21,050 --> 00:04:24,090
Don't worry about it, already! Things happen.

77
00:04:25,300 --> 00:04:29,300
So you're not mad at me for getting fired and everything?

78
00:04:29,470 --> 00:04:31,720
Joey, look at me.

79
00:04:32,270 --> 00:04:33,730
Look at me!

80
00:04:35,480 --> 00:04:37,900
Do I have lipstick on my teeth?

81
00:04:40,900 --> 00:04:41,980
No.

82
00:04:42,150 --> 00:04:43,440
Uh, can we get back to me?

83
00:04:43,610 --> 00:04:47,740
Look, honey. People get fired left and right in this business.

84
00:04:47,990 --> 00:04:50,910
I already got you an audition for Another World.

85
00:04:51,290 --> 00:04:52,490
All right!

86
00:04:53,620 --> 00:04:55,250
Cab Driver Number 2?

87
00:04:55,750 --> 00:04:57,120
You're welcome.

88
00:04:58,540 --> 00:05:01,300
But... But I was Dr. Drake Ramoray!

89
00:05:01,460 --> 00:05:05,010
How can I go from being a neurosurgeon to driving a cab?

90
00:05:05,260 --> 00:05:08,680
Things change. Roll with them.

91
00:05:08,850 --> 00:05:11,180
But this is a two-line part!

92
00:05:11,430 --> 00:05:13,560
It's like taking a step backwards.

93
00:05:13,770 --> 00:05:15,640
I'm not gonna do this.

94
00:05:17,060 --> 00:05:18,480
Joey.

95
00:05:18,690 --> 00:05:21,650
I'm gonna tell you the same thing I told Al Minza...

96
00:05:21,820 --> 00:05:24,190
and his pyramid of dogs.

97
00:05:25,450 --> 00:05:28,860
Take any job you can get, and don't make on the floor.

98
00:05:30,780 --> 00:05:32,410
I'm sorry.

99
00:05:32,990 --> 00:05:34,080
I'll see you.

100
00:05:35,620 --> 00:05:37,870
Uh, God!

101
00:05:38,370 --> 00:05:40,460
Oh, God! I mean, it's just so...

102
00:05:40,710 --> 00:05:41,920
Isn't it?

103
00:05:42,460 --> 00:05:45,210
Uh. This is like reading about my own life!

104
00:05:45,380 --> 00:05:49,260
This book could have been called Be Your Own Wind Keeper, Rachel!

105
00:05:50,180 --> 00:05:52,140
It wouldn't have sold a million copies...

106
00:05:52,350 --> 00:05:54,510
but it would've made a nice gift for you.

107
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:56,930
Hey, you guys.

108
00:05:57,100 --> 00:05:59,140
Uh, sweetie, we've gotta go.

109
00:05:59,310 --> 00:06:00,400
No!

110
00:06:02,520 --> 00:06:03,730
No? Heh.

111
00:06:03,900 --> 00:06:08,780
No! Why do we always have to do everything according to your timetable?

112
00:06:08,950 --> 00:06:12,240
Actually, it's the movie theater that has the timetable.

113
00:06:13,030 --> 00:06:14,910
It's so you don't miss the beginning.

114
00:06:16,580 --> 00:06:21,380
This isn't about the movie theater. This is about you stealing my wind.

115
00:06:23,420 --> 00:06:25,000
You go, girl!

116
00:06:27,920 --> 00:06:30,130
I can't pull that off, can I?

117
00:06:31,930 --> 00:06:34,930
Excuse me, your wind?

118
00:06:35,100 --> 00:06:36,600
Yes, my wind.

119
00:06:36,810 --> 00:06:39,640
How do you expect me to grow if you won't let me blow?

120
00:06:46,860 --> 00:06:48,900
You know I don't...

121
00:06:51,490 --> 00:06:53,700
have a problem with that.

122
00:07:01,370 --> 00:07:03,210
Okay.

123
00:07:04,590 --> 00:07:08,420
I just really need to be with myself right now.

124
00:07:09,510 --> 00:07:10,590
I'm sorry.

125
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:13,140
Uh, you're right.

126
00:07:13,300 --> 00:07:14,680
I don't have to apologize.

127
00:07:15,600 --> 00:07:17,180
Sorry. Damn it!

128
00:07:23,900 --> 00:07:25,480
What is it?

129
00:07:23,900 --> 00:07:25,480
I don't know.

130
00:07:25,730 --> 00:07:28,530
It's got all this stuff about wind and trees...

131
00:07:28,690 --> 00:07:30,280
and some sacred pool.

132
00:07:30,530 --> 00:07:33,740
I don't really get it. But she's pretty upset about it.

133
00:07:33,910 --> 00:07:36,740
This is why I don't date women who read.

134
00:07:38,450 --> 00:07:39,540
Uh-oh.

135
00:07:39,700 --> 00:07:41,040
What? What's that?

136
00:07:41,870 --> 00:07:43,370
It's my VISA bill.

137
00:07:43,620 --> 00:07:45,880
Envelope one of two.

138
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:50,760
That can't be good.

139
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:50,760
Open it, open it.

140
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:56,010
Oh, my God!

141
00:07:56,180 --> 00:07:58,260
Look at this! How did I spend so much money?

142
00:07:58,430 --> 00:08:02,600
Uh, that's just the minimum amount. That's your total.

143
00:08:02,850 --> 00:08:04,600
Aah!

144
00:08:04,770 --> 00:08:09,190
Whoa, whoa. Thirty-five hundred dollars at "Porcelain Safari"?

145
00:08:09,440 --> 00:08:10,780
My animals.

146
00:08:12,030 --> 00:08:15,160
The guy said they suited me. He spoke with an accent. I was confused.

147
00:08:15,320 --> 00:08:16,820
Oh.

148
00:08:17,330 --> 00:08:18,660
I don't know what to do!

149
00:08:18,910 --> 00:08:21,830
You can start by driving a cab on Another World.

150
00:08:22,080 --> 00:08:23,790
That audition?

151
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:25,750
That's a two-line part!

152
00:08:25,920 --> 00:08:30,090
Joey, you owe $1100 at "I Love Lucite."

153
00:08:30,250 --> 00:08:32,630
So What?

154
00:08:30,250 --> 00:08:32,630
So suck it up, man.

155
00:08:32,800 --> 00:08:34,380
It's a job. It's money.

156
00:08:34,550 --> 00:08:38,430
I don't need you getting judgmental and condescending and pedantic.

157
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:42,020
Toilet paper?

158
00:08:43,980 --> 00:08:46,900
I'm not being any of those things. I'm being realistic.

159
00:08:47,060 --> 00:08:49,980
You're supposed to be my friend!

160
00:08:47,060 --> 00:08:49,980
I am your friend.

161
00:08:50,150 --> 00:08:52,230
Then tell me things like, "You'll be fine."

162
00:08:52,490 --> 00:08:55,650
And "Something big's gonna come along. I know it!"

163
00:08:55,820 --> 00:08:57,160
But I don't know it.

164
00:08:57,370 --> 00:09:00,870
What I do know is you owe $2300 at "Isn't It Chromantic?"

165
00:09:02,410 --> 00:09:04,290
I'm aware of what I owe.

166
00:09:04,460 --> 00:09:08,290
Then get some sense. It took you 10 years to get that job.

167
00:09:08,460 --> 00:09:11,340
How long till you get another?

168
00:09:08,460 --> 00:09:11,340
I don't want to hear this!

169
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:12,590
I'm just saying...

170
00:09:12,800 --> 00:09:14,920
Well, don't "just say"!

171
00:09:16,680 --> 00:09:18,840
Oh. Maybe I should just go.

172
00:09:19,010 --> 00:09:21,680
Okay.

173
00:09:21,850 --> 00:09:23,390
I'll see you later.

174
00:09:24,770 --> 00:09:26,310
Just think about it.

175
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:28,400
I don't need to think about it!

176
00:09:28,650 --> 00:09:30,860
I was Dr. Drake Ramoray!

177
00:09:31,110 --> 00:09:32,900
That was huge!

178
00:09:33,190 --> 00:09:35,610
Big things are gonna happen! You'll see!

179
00:09:35,820 --> 00:09:36,900
Ross!

180
00:09:37,450 --> 00:09:38,950
You still there?

181
00:09:50,250 --> 00:09:52,460
Hey, pal.

182
00:09:54,340 --> 00:09:55,840
What are you still doing here?

183
00:09:56,010 --> 00:09:58,970
Uh, just some basic dehydrating of fruits and vegetables.

184
00:09:59,140 --> 00:10:01,680
Man alive, this thing's fantastic!

185
00:10:02,890 --> 00:10:04,760
Aren't you forgetting anything?

186
00:10:04,930 --> 00:10:07,100
Oh, yeah. I got us a new goldfish.

187
00:10:07,310 --> 00:10:09,230
He's feistier than the last one.

188
00:10:09,400 --> 00:10:12,520
Maybe because the last one was made by Pepperidge Farm.

189
00:10:14,030 --> 00:10:17,740
Eddie, isn't there something else you're supposed to be doing now?

190
00:10:17,900 --> 00:10:20,410
Not unless it's got something to do with dehydrating.

191
00:10:20,570 --> 00:10:22,410
Because right now, I'm a dehydrating maniac!

192
00:10:26,330 --> 00:10:27,870
I thought we had a deal.

193
00:10:28,040 --> 00:10:29,710
I thought by the time I got...

194
00:10:29,870 --> 00:10:31,000
You know what that is?

195
00:10:31,750 --> 00:10:32,920
Your last roommate's kidney?

196
00:10:34,920 --> 00:10:36,670
That's a tomato. Ha, ha.

197
00:10:36,920 --> 00:10:38,760
This one definitely goes in the display.

198
00:10:43,010 --> 00:10:44,100
It's in the display.

199
00:10:48,850 --> 00:10:51,770
Hey, Gunther. Let me get a, uh, lemonade to go.

200
00:10:52,190 --> 00:10:53,360
Lemonade?

201
00:10:53,520 --> 00:10:55,070
You okay, man?

202
00:10:56,030 --> 00:10:57,570
Ahh, it's career stuff.

203
00:10:57,820 --> 00:11:00,490
They killed off my character on the show.

204
00:11:00,660 --> 00:11:03,410
Oh, that's too bad. How'd they do it?

205
00:11:03,570 --> 00:11:05,530
I fell down an elevator shaft.

206
00:11:05,700 --> 00:11:07,870
Oh. That sucks.

207
00:11:08,120 --> 00:11:09,750
I was buried in an avalanche.

208
00:11:11,290 --> 00:11:12,370
What?

209
00:11:12,540 --> 00:11:15,130
I used to be Brice on All My Children.

210
00:11:37,070 --> 00:11:38,730
Why must everybody watch me sleep?

211
00:11:39,280 --> 00:11:41,610
There'll be no more watching me sleep! No more...

212
00:11:41,780 --> 00:11:42,860
watching!

213
00:11:43,070 --> 00:11:44,360
Neh.

214
00:11:48,870 --> 00:11:52,710
I got some great stuff to dehydrate. Grapes, apricots.

215
00:11:52,870 --> 00:11:56,420
I thought it'd be cool to see what happens with these water balloons.

216
00:11:56,590 --> 00:11:59,670
Get out. Get out, get out, get out!

217
00:12:01,300 --> 00:12:02,920
You! Move out!

218
00:12:03,180 --> 00:12:07,260
Take your fruit! Your stupid small fruit and get out!

219
00:12:07,430 --> 00:12:09,430
You want me to move out? Uh...

220
00:12:13,270 --> 00:12:15,150
Uh-huh.

221
00:12:16,480 --> 00:12:17,690
Heh. I, uh...

222
00:12:17,900 --> 00:12:22,190
Ooh! Ha, ha. I gotta tell you, that's kind of out of the blue, don't you think?

223
00:12:23,740 --> 00:12:25,200
This is not out of the blue!

224
00:12:25,450 --> 00:12:27,570
This is smack-dab in the middle of the blue!

225
00:12:27,950 --> 00:12:29,410
Whoa!

226
00:12:30,120 --> 00:12:32,080
Relax! Take it easy, buddy!

227
00:12:32,330 --> 00:12:34,080
You don't have to tell me twice.

228
00:12:34,250 --> 00:12:37,710
God, I mean, someone will be by for my stuff.

229
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:46,220
If you think I'm leaving you alone with my fish, you're insane!

230
00:12:47,510 --> 00:12:48,600
You want some help?

231
00:12:48,760 --> 00:12:50,680
No help required, chico!

232
00:12:59,400 --> 00:13:02,730
All the way to the airport, huh? You know, that's over 30 miles.

233
00:13:02,900 --> 00:13:06,240
That's gonna cost you about so bucks.

234
00:13:06,410 --> 00:13:08,660
Excuse me, that's "50" bucks.

235
00:13:09,700 --> 00:13:12,830
Fifty dollars.

236
00:13:13,040 --> 00:13:17,710
Oh. You know what it is? It's smudgy because they're fax pages.

237
00:13:17,960 --> 00:13:20,840
When I was on Days ofour Lives as Dr. Drake Ramoray...

238
00:13:21,880 --> 00:13:24,340
they'd send over the whole script on real paper.

239
00:13:24,510 --> 00:13:27,430
Ha, ha. That's great.

240
00:13:27,590 --> 00:13:31,680
If you wanted to expand this scene, like have the cab crash...

241
00:13:31,890 --> 00:13:33,770
I could attend to the victims...

242
00:13:33,970 --> 00:13:36,060
because I have a background in medical acting.

243
00:13:37,600 --> 00:13:39,270
Okay, listen, thanks for coming in.

244
00:13:39,440 --> 00:13:43,650
No. Uh, don't thank me for coming in. Uh, at least let me finish.

245
00:13:46,490 --> 00:13:48,990
Uh, we could take the expressway...

246
00:13:49,160 --> 00:13:53,660
but, uh, this time of day you're better off taking the budge.

247
00:13:56,660 --> 00:13:59,540
You were going for bridge there, weren't you?

248
00:14:00,540 --> 00:14:01,630
I'll have a good day.

249
00:14:06,550 --> 00:14:08,470
Okay, question number 28.

250
00:14:08,840 --> 00:14:12,760
Have you ever allowed a lightning-bearer to take your wind?

251
00:14:13,560 --> 00:14:15,270
I would have to say no.

252
00:14:17,730 --> 00:14:19,520
And I would have to say, "Pah, ha."

253
00:14:20,560 --> 00:14:21,650
What?

254
00:14:21,810 --> 00:14:23,570
Do you not remember the puppet guy?

255
00:14:23,730 --> 00:14:28,740
You totally let him wash his feet in the pool of your inner power.

256
00:14:30,240 --> 00:14:31,990
And his puppet too!

257
00:14:32,160 --> 00:14:34,370
Well, at least I didn't let some guy...

258
00:14:34,620 --> 00:14:37,500
into the forest of my righteous truth on the first date!

259
00:14:41,880 --> 00:14:43,210
- Who?     - Paul!

260
00:14:43,420 --> 00:14:44,790
Oh!

261
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:48,170
Okay, okay, moving on, moving on. Next question.

262
00:14:48,420 --> 00:14:49,880
Okay, number 29.

263
00:14:50,130 --> 00:14:53,050
Have you ever betrayed another goddess..._x000D_

264
00:14:53,300 --> 00:14:56,220
for a lightning-bearer? Okay, number 30.

265
00:14:56,390 --> 00:14:59,310
Whoa, whoa, whoa! Let's go back to 29.

266
